  "slug": "pharmacology-validation-bc48",
  "summary": "Bulk and single-cell transcriptomic models are increasingly paired with survival endpoints across multiple cancer types in recent publications.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of transcriptomic and multi-omics data to derive prognostic or risk-stratification models across diverse carcinomas, including cervical, lung, gastric, ovarian, and breast cancers. Several items build signatures from public TCGA and GEO cohorts, such as the nucleotide metabolism subtype signature in lung adenocarcinoma and the anoikis-related gene model in gastric cancer, while others add single-cell or spatial resolution, as with epithelial subcluster epi8 in cervical lesions and longitudinal neuroblastoma analysis. A second strand links candidate genes to metabolic mechanisms, including ALYREF stabilization of PKM2 mRNA, MIB2-mediated ubiquitination of HK2, and hnRNPC-dependent stabilization of BCAT1. Newer elements include a radiogenomic link between CT radiomics and transcription factor regulatory states in NSCLC and a serum proteomic TIPscore for oligometastatic gastric cancer. Most signatures are established in retrospective public cohorts.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-03"
}
